1
00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.710
In this lesson, you will learn all the
foundations of LLM Data pre-processing,

2
00:00:14.939 --> 00:00:15.599
Let's dive in.

3
00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:18.039
Before you learn how to
pre-process specific data types,

4
00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:27.192
First, it's important to understand
retrieval augmented generation or RAG.

5
00:00:27.192 --> 00:00:31.695
This is a technique for grounding LLM
responses on validated external information,

6
00:00:31.695 --> 00:00:33.571
such as your corporate information.

7
00:00:33.571 --> 00:00:36.572
This information can be
contained in various document

8
00:00:36.572 --> 00:00:39.950
types such as emails or
PDFs or PowerPoint slides.

9
00:00:40.369 --> 00:00:42.929
Conceptually, RAG
applications load this external context

10
00:00:42.929 --> 00:00:45.850
into a database and then
retrieve that content,

11
00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:50.600
insert it into a prompt, and
then pass that prompt to an LLM.

12
00:00:51.829 --> 00:00:57.506
That way, by the time a
question or a prompt gets to the LLM,

13
00:00:57.506 --> 00:01:02.777
it contains external information that
the LLM can use to construct its response.

14
00:01:02.777 --> 00:01:03.993
Within industry applications,

15
00:01:03.993 --> 00:01:06.426
this is extremely
relevant because organizational

16
00:01:06.426 --> 00:01:09.670
data exists in a wide variety of formats.

17
00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:12.790
Pre-processing
documents includes a few steps.

18
00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:16.069
First, you need to
extract the document content.

19
00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:18.620
This is the text
content from the documents

20
00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:20.730
that can be used to
construct your prompts.

21
00:01:21.129 --> 00:01:24.840
There's other information
that's important to extract as well,

22
00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:26.069
including document elements.

23
00:01:26.769 --> 00:01:29.859
These are the basic
building blocks of a document,

24
00:01:29.859 --> 00:01:32.609
such as titles, narrative
text, lists, and tables.

25
00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:35.683
You can use these
document elements for a variety

26
00:01:35.683 --> 00:01:38.362
of tasks that are
important to RAG applications,

27
00:01:38.362 --> 00:01:40.036
such as chunking and filtering.

28
00:01:40.036 --> 00:01:43.719
You will learn more about these
operations later in the course.

29
00:01:44.299 --> 00:01:47.629
In addition, it is important
to extract element metadata,

30
00:01:47.629 --> 00:01:50.219
such as page number or file type.

31
00:01:50.799 --> 00:01:54.530
Later in the course,
you will learn how to use

32
00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:56.780
this information when
performing hybrid search,

33
00:01:56.780 --> 00:02:00.510
which allows you to filter the
information that you extract

34
00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:03.500
from a vector database when
constructing your prompt.

35
00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:08.349
So why is data pre-processing hard?
Well, there are a few reasons. First,

36
00:02:08.349 --> 00:02:10.840
different document types
have different content cues.

37
00:02:11.479 --> 00:02:13.379
So an HTML file, for example,

38
00:02:13.379 --> 00:02:16.539
may use tag names to
give an indication as to

39
00:02:16.539 --> 00:02:20.030
whether a piece of text
is a title or a list.

40
00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:24.270
PDFs will have visual cues,
so an entirely different cue.

41
00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:28.280
And so being able to
pre-process all of these different

42
00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:31.099
document types in a
common manner requires you

43
00:02:31.099 --> 00:02:33.560
to understand how
different document types indicate

44
00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:36.719
what a different element
is in that document type.

45
00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:39.960
Second, documents come
in a variety of formats,

46
00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:42.580
and you need to
standardize these so that your

47
00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:44.919
application can process
them in the same way.

48
00:02:50.379 --> 00:02:50.599
So ideally, your application just needs
to know what the external information is.

49
00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:55.120
It doesn't necessarily care whether
the source document is an HTML or a PDF.

50
00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:01.020
By standardizing these documents,

51
00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:01.479
you can treat all of these documents in
the same way in your end user application.

52
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:07.740
However, standardizing
is hard because, again,

53
00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:08.610
these documents have
different formats and different

54
00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:09.599
cues as to what the element types are.

55
00:03:09.599 --> 00:03:13.750
Additionally, different documents have
different formats. And so, for example,

56
00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:15.900
a journal article and
a form are different.

57
00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:17.560
And so data pre-processing techniques need

58
00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:24.530
Finally, you need to
understand information

59
00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:27.330
about document
structure in order to extract

60
00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:32.150
meaningful metadata that you can use for
various operations in RAG applications,

61
00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:36.560
such as filtering, that you'll
learn about later in this course.

62
00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:40.340
All in all, there are a lot of moving
parts when it comes to data preprocessing.

63
00:03:40.919 --> 00:03:42.210
And it's not very simple,

64
00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:45.800
but it's an important part of getting
up and running with a RAG application.

65
00:03:46.479 --> 00:03:50.110
In the next lesson, we'll
jump in and we'll learn

66
00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:53.009
how to pre-process a few
different document types,

67
00:03:53.009 --> 00:03:54.819
including PowerPoints, HTML, and PDFs.